{
  "case_id": "2004-04-169",
  "document_hash": "0fd3acd3905183267b9a327fdc43f1bbff42a486d63308fef30bc7e4da84ff6c",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Christopher G. Browning, Jr.",
      "case_id": "2004-04-169",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0fd3acd3905183267b9a327fdc43f1bbff42a486d63308fef30bc7e4da84ff6c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0fd3acd39051-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. BROWNING: Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: The issue in this case is whether Congress expressly provided for a limitations period for retaliatory discharge action under the Federal False Claims Act."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States (amicus)",
      "case_id": "2004-04-169",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0fd3acd3905183267b9a327fdc43f1bbff42a486d63308fef30bc7e4da84ff6c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0fd3acd39051-0000-0002",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The Government in its amicus brief argues that"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Christopher G. Browning, Jr.",
      "case_id": "2004-04-169",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0fd3acd3905183267b9a327fdc43f1bbff42a486d63308fef30bc7e4da84ff6c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0fd3acd39051-0000-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The retaliatory discharge provision, section 3730(h) of the False Claims Act, not only protects people who blow the whistle, the initial whistleblower, but it expressly protects people who testify at trial."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2004-04-169",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0fd3acd3905183267b9a327fdc43f1bbff42a486d63308fef30bc7e4da84ff6c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0fd3acd39051-0000-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "but are there any actual cases where the retaliation in fact fell outside the 6-year, dated from the submission of the false claim?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2004-04-169",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0fd3acd3905183267b9a327fdc43f1bbff42a486d63308fef30bc7e4da84ff6c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0fd3acd39051-0000-0006",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Work Right Institute points out, an organization that's designed to protect the human rights of employees, an organization that's a spin-off of the American Civil Liberties Union, that organization clearly views the interpretation advocated by the respondents and adopted by the Fourth Circuit as detrimental by employees."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States (amicus)",
      "case_id": "2004-04-169",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0fd3acd3905183267b9a327fdc43f1bbff42a486d63308fef30bc7e4da84ff6c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0fd3acd39051-0000-0008",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 16,
        "start": 16
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Government's brief is they want to modify the language of section 3731(b) by inserting the phrase alleged before the phrase, violation of section 3729."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondent",
      "case_id": "2004-04-169",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0fd3acd3905183267b9a327fdc43f1bbff42a486d63308fef30bc7e4da84ff6c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0fd3acd39051-0001-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 19,
        "start": 19
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The respondent would argue in their brief that you start it running from when they believed that the violation took place. So your -- the respondent is arguing you're looking to the mental impression of the litigant that would be the triggering device for the limitations period."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondent",
      "case_id": "2004-04-169",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0fd3acd3905183267b9a327fdc43f1bbff42a486d63308fef30bc7e4da84ff6c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0fd3acd39051-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The text of this statute could not be more clear on this issue. A retaliation claim is a civil action under section 3730, and a civil action under 3730 is subject to a 6-year limitations period."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Rehnquist",
      "case_id": "2004-04-169",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0fd3acd3905183267b9a327fdc43f1bbff42a486d63308fef30bc7e4da84ff6c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0fd3acd39051-0001-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE REHNQUIST: Very well, Mr. Browning."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2004-04-169",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0fd3acd3905183267b9a327fdc43f1bbff42a486d63308fef30bc7e4da84ff6c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0fd3acd39051-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Do you -- do you know of -- of any other situation in which a -- a time limit is imposed that has nothing whatever to do with the -- with the act that the individual is complaining about, nothing whatever to do with the act --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondent",
      "case_id": "2004-04-169",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0fd3acd3905183267b9a327fdc43f1bbff42a486d63308fef30bc7e4da84ff6c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0fd3acd39051-0001-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Having the same trigger for all three 3730 actions means the limitations period starts for all three at the same time. This makes it easier for the whistleblower to bring both their qui tam and retaliation actions together."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Douglas Hallward-DrieMeier",
      "case_id": "2004-04-169",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0fd3acd3905183267b9a327fdc43f1bbff42a486d63308fef30bc7e4da84ff6c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0fd3acd39051-0001-0003",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 29,
        "start": 29
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Section 3731(b) provides the statute of limitations for, quote, a civil action under section 3730."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2004-04-169",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0fd3acd3905183267b9a327fdc43f1bbff42a486d63308fef30bc7e4da84ff6c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0fd3acd39051-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 36,
        "start": 36
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: So that -- that could exclude even a retaliation action by an individual who -- who doesn't -- who doesn't know when the -- when the individual -- the official of the United States found out about those facts. That -- that doesn't seem to me very reasonable."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Government",
      "case_id": "2004-04-169",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0fd3acd3905183267b9a327fdc43f1bbff42a486d63308fef30bc7e4da84ff6c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0fd3acd39051-0002-0004",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 37,
        "start": 37
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "But in the Bay Area Laundry case, the Court considered and rejected a virtually identical argument."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Souter",
      "case_id": "2004-04-169",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0fd3acd3905183267b9a327fdc43f1bbff42a486d63308fef30bc7e4da84ff6c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0fd3acd39051-0002-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The -- Justice Souter asked a question about statistics and the frequency with which the claims are litigated together."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Government",
      "case_id": "2004-04-169",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0fd3acd3905183267b9a327fdc43f1bbff42a486d63308fef30bc7e4da84ff6c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0fd3acd39051-0002-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "the analogous statute of limitations under Florida law -- it's the Florida State False Claims Act which has a retaliation provision -- would be 180 days. 180 days is far too short to put together the complicated qui tam complaint that is called for under the False Claims Act."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Rehnquist",
      "case_id": "2004-04-169",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0fd3acd3905183267b9a327fdc43f1bbff42a486d63308fef30bc7e4da84ff6c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0fd3acd39051-0002-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE REHNQUIST: Well, apparently Florida didn't think so."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Government",
      "case_id": "2004-04-169",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0fd3acd3905183267b9a327fdc43f1bbff42a486d63308fef30bc7e4da84ff6c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0fd3acd39051-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 41,
        "start": 41
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The Government argues that there will be problems that will arise from splitting the qui tam action and the retaliatory discharge action. And one of the examples that the Government use -- uses is the public disclosure doctrine which is set out in section 3730(e)(4)(A) of the False Claims Act."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2004-04-169",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0fd3acd3905183267b9a327fdc43f1bbff42a486d63308fef30bc7e4da84ff6c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0fd3acd39051-0002-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 41,
        "start": 41
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The public disclosure doctrine, however, is a red herring here because the public disclosure doctrine is designed to keep -- to avoid parasitic lawsuits where information is in the -- the public domain, somebody taking that information, and then filing a -- a qui tam lawsuit."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2004-04-169",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "0fd3acd3905183267b9a327fdc43f1bbff42a486d63308fef30bc7e4da84ff6c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-0fd3acd39051-0002-0008",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "ERISA is a statute that has essentially a retaliatory discharge provision as well in section 510 of ERISA, 29 U.S.C. 1140. And courts -- the Federal courts have consistently held that there is no express limitations period for section 510, so we have to look to the limitations period under the most closely analogous State law cause of action."
    }
  ],
  "schema_version": "1.0.0"
}
